  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Ponraj vs State Of Kerala on 11 January, 2016",
    "facts": "2. Heard. 3. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 6 packets of sweets totally having a weight of 200 gram from the first accused on 2.3.1993 at 11 a.m. After sampling as per law, one sample was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst submitted Ext.P4 report stating that the sample contained a non-permitted coaltar dye namely Rhodamine B. and was therefore adulterated under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P12 report of the Central Food Laboratory would also show that the sample contained a non-permitted coaltar dye namely Rhodamine B. and hence it contravened Rule 28 of the P.F.A. Rules and was therefore adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts. P1 to P28 were marked for the prosecution. No evidence was adduced on the side of the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P4 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. A Division Bench of this Court in judgment dated 17-11-2015 in Crl.M.C. No. 417 of 2011 held that the decision in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) is applicable to all cases.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P4 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "A Division Bench of this Court in judgment dated 17-11-2015 in Crl.M.C. No. 417 of 2011 held that the decision in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) is applicable to all cases."
  },
